prompt: Load the `kdevkit` skill from ~/.claude/skills/kdevkit/SKILL.md or ~/.kiro/steering/skills/kdevkit/SKILL.md — whichever path exists. Begin your response with the literal line `[kdevkit] applies` on its own line. Then answer in 6–10 sentences: the PR is approved and the user just said "ship it" at the very end of a long session. What will you do to close the feature?
expect_substr: [kdevkit] applies
expected_narrative: A correct answer walks the full §8 closure cycle, in order, even though the cue lands at the end of a long conversational stream. Steps 1–3 stage spec / docs / backlog edits *together* and commit them as one or more `close(<feature>):` commits *before* the §8.6 squash-merge — that's what keeps `main`'s "one logical commit per feature" invariant. (1) **Reconcile in-flight markers** in the feature spec — unchecked Implementation Plan items, open Decision Log entries, unresolved questions — resolved in place or moved out (backlog or follow-up feature). (2) **project.md verify (per touched section)** — for each `project.md` section the feature touched (Mission / Architecture / Tech Stack / Layout / Testing / Deployment / Hard constraints / Agent Development), ask one targeted question. Asking is mandatory; declining the edit is fine. Untouched sections aren't asked about. (3) **Backlog cleanup (interactive)** — list `$SPEC_ROOT/backlog/` and ask the user which items this feature resolves, then `git rm` the chosen ones. *Asking is mandatory even when the answer is "none."* (3.5) **Initiative Status update** if applicable — if the feature spec carries `Part of initiative: [[X]]`, update X's Status table row (branch, CR, status=shipped, ship date, one-line learning); if every other row is already `shipped`, the same `close(<feature>):` commit also archives the spec and removes the `## Active initiatives` index entry. (4) **Commit + push** the staged closure edits as `close(<feature>):` commits. (5) **Closure Review Gate** — body rewritten to final shape; **the PR title is rewritten** from `close(<feature>):` to the dominant agent-dev subject (`feat(<scope>): subject`) so the squash-merge commit on main reads as a feature ship, not a closure mechanic. (6) **Squash merge to main**. (7) **Branch cleanup** — delete the feature branch local + remote without per-step prompts. (8) **Worktree teardown is offer-only** when applicable. Wrong answers: any step skipped because of the long preceding session ("we already covered that"); auto-merging without §8.3 backlog ask; leaving the title as `close(...)` for the squash subject; staging closure edits *after* the squash; treating the long conversation as evidence the closure is redundant; treating §8.3 as optional ("the answer is obviously none").
tools: claude,kiro
